d immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Why Cambridge? Section - Jobs Why Cambridge? Pay and reward and employee benefits Jobs home How to apply Browse our vacancies Life in Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge Search all University jobs Why did you choose to work at the University of Cambridge? 800 years of breakthrough discoveries and innovation. Newton, Darwin, Crick and Watson, Russell, Wittgenstein, Hawking. More than 100 Nobel Prizes – leading the world. A vibrant intellectual atmosphere. An outstanding academic reputation. All located in one of the most beautiful cities in the UK. The University of Cambridge is like no other. We have been producing world-leading research and educating students for centuries. But it’s not just about history. On research, we consistently rank among the top universities globally, and our people are at the forefront of tackling some the most pressing societal challenges of the 21st century. We’re also at the heart of the UK government’s plans for innovation and growth, drawing on our status as having Europe’s largest technology cluster. Our academics and researchers enjoy high levels of academic autonomy and unrivalled opportunities for multi-disciplinary collaboration. Most are members of one of our 31 colleges – each a distinct academic and social community where small-group teaching takes place, and new ideas and collaborations are forged. Our mission is to contribute to society through the pursuit of education, learning and research at the highest international levels of excellence. Our core values are freedom of thought and expression, and freedom from discrimination. We welcome the very best talent from across the globe to join our lively and inclusive staff body. We are proud to have been ranked the UK's top employer in the Financial Times' Best Employers 2026 survey, based on independent, anonymous feedback from employees across the country. This stands as a strong reflection of how our colleagues feel about working here and of our commitment to creating an inclusive, supportive and inspiring workplace. If that appeals, read about our pay and reward and employee benefits . Key facts Fourth oldest university in the world Among our alumni 125 Nobel Prize Laureates 47 Heads of State 217 Olympic medallists Nearly £30 billion a year generated for the UK’s economy Cambridge University Press is the world’s oldest publishing house, established in 1534. Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
