    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/cutover-manager-fixed-term-ag50759": {
      "title": "Breadcrumb",
      "description": "Cutover Manager (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Cutover Manager (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Cutover Manager (Fixed Term) The Cutover Manager plays a critical role in ensuring the programme successfully transitions from implementation into live operation. Your leadership will help deliver a smooth, controlled and well-coordinated deployment, ensuring business readiness, minimising operational risk, and providing confidence that every element of the programme has been planned and executed to the highest standard. Do you have the ability to coordinate multiple workstreams, manage competing priorities, and ensure flawless execution when it matters most? We are looking for an experienced Cutover Manager to lead the planning, coordination and execution of programme deployment activities for a major transformation programme. This is a pivotal role where success depends on your ability to bring structure to complexity, maintain momentum, and ensure every detail has been considered before, during and after go-live. As Cutover Manager, you will have overall responsibility for developing and delivering the programme's cutover strategy and managing the end-to-end deployment lifecycle. From planning and dry runs through to production build and final go-live, you will ensure every activity is carefully coordinated, risks are proactively managed, and operational readiness is achieved across the organisation. This role demands someone with outstanding organisational skills, exceptional attention to detail, and the confidence to challenge, influence and collaborate across technical and business teams to deliver a seamless transition. We're looking for someone who combines excellent programme delivery skills with a meticulous eye for detail.  You'll have: Significant experience managing cutover, deployment or transition activities within large, complex transformation programmes. Outstanding organisational and planning skills, with the ability to manage numerous interdependent activities simultaneously. Exceptional attention to detail, ensuring nothing is overlooked in highly complex deployment plans. Proven experience developing and managing detailed cutover plans across multiple environments. Experience managing risks, issues, dependencies and critical decision points throughout programme delivery. Excellent communication skills with the ability to coordinate diverse technical and business teams under pressure. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Funding for this post is available for 12 months. If you would like more information about this role, informal enquiries can be directed by email to Michelle Bond via Michelle.Bond@admin.cam.ac.uk If you have any questions the application process, please contact a Recruitment Administrator at Finhr_recruitment@admin.cam.ac.uk . The closing date for applications is 09 September 2026. The interviews date for the role are planned 22 September 2026. Please quote reference AG50759 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check, a health assessment and a security check. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information - Cutover Manager Key information Department/location Finance Division Salary £63,606-£67,468 Reference AG50759 Category Academic-related Date published 14 August 2026 Closing date 9 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/de-ying-research-associate-and-curator-contemporary-art-da50470": {
      "title": "De Ying Research Associate and Curator, Contemporary Art",
      "description": "De Ying Research Associate and Curator, Contemporary Art | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs De Ying Research Associate and Curator, Contemporary Art Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs De Ying Research Associate and Curator, Contemporary Art The Fitzwilliam Museum: Opening up the past to transform our futures. As the principal museum of the University of Cambridge and the largest cultural venue in the region, The Fitzwilliam Museum acts as a crucial bridge between the University, the City and the rest of the world, and has an international reputation for extraordinary research, world class exhibitions, award-winning public programmes and significant collections spanning many cultures and centuries. The Hamilton Kerr Institute (HKI) is one of the world's leading centres for teaching and research in conservation and heritage science. We invite applicants to provide curatorial expertise in the delivery of an ambitious programme of exhibitions, while contributing to and developing research on the collection. Working collaboratively with colleagues across the Museum, the postholder will help deliver contemporary projects in line with institutional processes while bringing creativity and fresh thinking to their implementation. The role involves supporting and undertaking a broad range of activities. These include liaising with multiple stakeholders, such as living artists, conducting collection and loans research, contributing to administration and drafting interpretive texts. The postholder will be based in and expected to contribute as a member of the Curatorial department but will also work closely with teams across the Museum, including Audience and Brand, Exhibitions, Development, Participation & Learning and Public Programmes. The Project Curator: Contemporary Art will have the opportunity to contribute to wider research, build partnerships across the University and participate in research networks. We particularly encourage applicants who will seek to bring new perspectives to the collection, including from under-represented audiences and through engagement with collections' diverse histories. In return, we offer a supportive and inclusive workplace, a generous pension scheme, enhanced family leave, substantial holiday allowance, and a range of employee benefits, including retail discounts. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 3 years in the first instance. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check and a security check. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Interviews for the role will be held: week commencing 19th October 2026 Please quote reference DA50470 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information De Ying Research Associate and Curator, Contemporary Art Key information Department/location Fitzwilliam Museum Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference DA50470 Category Research Date published 20 August 2026 Closing date 27 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/deputy-business-and-operations-manager-rg50741": {
      "title": "Breadcrumb",
      "description": "Deputy Business and Operations Manager | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Deputy Business and Operations Manager Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Deputy Business and Operations Manager We are seeking to recruit an enthusiastic Deputy Business and Operations Manager to work within the Institute of Metabolic Science. This is a busy position working in a dynamic environment supporting the Business and Operations Manager, the Head of Department and Head of Epidemiology in managing the Professional Services Team across the Institute of Metabolic Science ¿ Metabolic Research Laboratories and the Institute of Metabolic Science Epidemiology within the Institute of Metabolic Science. With a key focus on finance management and research grant administration, the post holder will develop, review and implement administrative structures and systems as appropriate to ensure the effective delivery of services. They will identify areas of service that need improvement and implement changes to processes, polices and working practices. They will support the implementation of vital University of Cambridge transformation projects in the Institute such as the Finance Transformation Programme. They will also support the Business and Operations Manager in the day-to-day operational management of a large administrative and support team, delivering professional services to Principal Investigators and their groups across the IMS.  The IMS Professional Services team supports over 300 staff and nearly 100 PhD and MPhil students.  The role is based on the Cambridge Biomedical Campus within Addenbrookes Hospital. Candidates must have a degree level education or equivalent, with substantial finance and grant administration experience at a senior level. You will have excellent organisational skills, the ability to work on your own initiative, prioritise work, work to tight deadlines and have excellent people management and communication skills. For an informal discussion about the post, please contact Sophie Beresford, sb2751@cam.ac.uk Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and a CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. The closing date for applications is Sunday 13 September 2026 The interview date for the role is to be confirmed Please quote reference RG50741 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Clinical Biochemistry Salary £42,254-£56,535 Reference RG50741 Category Academic-related Date published 13 August 2026 Closing date 13 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/dobson-professorship-of-materials-science-and-metallurgy-lj50922": {
      "title": "Breadcrumb",
      "description": "Dobson Professorship of Materials Science and Metallurgy | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Dobson Professorship of Materials Science and Metallurgy Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Dobson Professorship of Materials Science and Metallurgy The Board of Electors to the Dobson Professorship of Materials Science and Metallurgy invite applications for this Professorship from persons whose work falls within the field of the Professorship, to take up appointment in 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter.\nThe Professorship benefits from a permanent endowment that provides research support and an exceptional opportunity to build a world-leading research group.\nCandidates will have an outstanding research record and a sustained international reputation in energy materials and materials discovery. The successful candidate will establish an ambitious research programme focused on accelerating materials discovery, development and translation of functional electronic materials and devices. The Department is particularly interested in automation, computational materials discovery, and low-power electronic devices for information communication technologies. Research may be experimental, computational or integrated, but should present a compelling vision for addressing challenges in energy materials and technologies. The successful candidate will provide intellectual and strategic leadership, build major interdisciplinary collaborations across the Department, School and wider University, and engage effectively with external partners. They should possess the vision, leadership, experience and enthusiasm to sustain and extend Cambridge's leading research presence, and will hold a PhD or equivalent postgraduate qualification.\nStandard professorial duties include teaching and research, examining, supervision and administration. The Professor will be based in Cambridge. A competitive salary will be offered. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Further information is available at: www.hr.admin.cam.ac.uk/professorships or from Human Resources, University Offices, The Old Schools, Cambridge, CB2 1TT (email: professorships@admin.cam.ac.uk ).\nApplications, consisting of a letter of application, a statement of current and future research plans, a curriculum vitae and a publications list, together with details of three referees, should be made online no later than 30 November 2026.\nInformal enquiries may be directed to the Head of Department, Professor Jason Robinson, hod@msm.cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference LJ50922 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Materials Science and Metallurgy Salary Reference LJ50922 Category Professorships and Directorships Date published 2 September 2026 Closing date 30 November 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/due-diligence-specialist-2-years-fixed-term-ew49670": {
      "title": "Due Diligence Specialist (2 Years Fixed term)",
      "description": "Due Diligence Specialist (2 Years Fixed term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Due Diligence Specialist (2 Years Fixed term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Due Diligence Specialist (2 Years Fixed term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 2 years in the first instance. We are looking for an experienced research and due diligence professional to join the Research Office's Due Diligence team as part of the Governance, Risk and Compliance function. The Research Services Division takes a strategic role in the oversight and development of all aspects of the University's research activities. The Due Diligence team help support researchers working with UK and international organisations to ensure their partnerships do not expose the University to risks, which could undermine the University's academic standing and delivery of its research mission. What will you do? As a Due Diligence Specialist, you will provide targeted analysis to identify and mitigate potential risks associated with third party research engagements and help ensure compliance with relevant legislation associated international engagement and research security. The role will offer the successful applicant the opportunity to: Help safeguard the reputation of the University of Cambridge; Support researchers at the forefront of scientific advancement to navigate complex regulation and risks that come with international research collaboration in an evolving geopolitical landscape; Build specialist research expertise and apply to various regions and sectors; Be part of a growing team in the Governance, Risk and Compliance function; Work within one of the most dynamic and varied research centres in the world. Skills We are looking for a skilled researcher with an interest in legal frameworks and international affairs with excellent analytical and drafting skills The successful candidate should have experience using open-source research tools such as LexisNexis and Factiva, as well as regional and language-specific databases and media sources. Location The University is supportive of hybrid working, where some work is undertaken on university premises and some in a remote working environment. The aim of our approach is to enable as many staff as possible to work in a hybrid way if they wish and where their role allows, with the majority of time spent on-site. The hours of work for the position are full-time with in-person working on Mondays and Thursdays. Informal enquiries should be directed to Rebecca Reid ( rebecca.reid@admin.cam.ac.uk ). Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working (min 80%) or other flexible working arrangements. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please see the Further Particulars document for more details of this vacancy. Your application should clearly set out how you meet the criteria listed in the person specification (the skills and experience required for the role) along with relevant examples. Candidates will be shortlisted based on these criteria. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. The closing date is Sunday 18th October 2026.  It is anticipated interviews will be held in person in Cambridge on the 2nd November 2026. Questions about the application process should be addressed to the Research Office Recruitment team at crorecruitment@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference EW49670 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Cambridge Research Office Salary £35,608-£46,049 Reference EW49670 Category Academic-related Date published 1 September 2026 Closing date 19 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
