      "description": "Kidani Professorship of Integrated Cancer Medicine | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Kidani Professorship of Integrated Cancer Medicine Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Kidani Professorship of Integrated Cancer Medicine The Board of Electors to the Kidani Professorship of Integrated Cancer Medicine invite applications for this Professorship from persons whose work falls within the general field of the Professorship to take up appointment in 2027. Candidates will have an outstanding research record of international stature in leading multidisciplinary translational cancer research involving advanced computational approaches and the vision, leadership, experience and enthusiasm to help us realise an ambitious vision for digital enabled detection, prevention and treatment of cancer in the new Cambridge Cancer Research Hospital.  They will hold a PhD or equivalent postgraduate qualification. Standard professorial duties include leading a research team, postgraduate teaching, and supervision as well as some administration.  The Professor will be based in the Early Cancer Institute, University of Cambridge and space will be available in the CCRH from 2031.  A competitive salary and a generous start-up package will be offered. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Further information is available at http://www.hr.admin.cam.ac.uk/professorships or contact the Human Resources, University Offices, The Old Schools, Cambridge, CB2 1TT, (email: professorships@admin.cam.ac.uk ). Applications, consisting of a letter of application, a statement of current and future research vision and plans, a curriculum vitae and a publications list, along with details of three referees should be made online no later than 26 October 2026. Informal enquiries may be directed to Professor Rebecca Fitzgerald, Director of the Early Cancer Institute and academic lead for the CCRH : rcf29@cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference RD50746 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Oncology Salary Reference RD50746 Category Professorships and Directorships Date published 13 August 2026 Closing date 26 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/llm-and-mcl-admissions-administrator-jk50816": {
      "title": "Breadcrumb",
      "description": "LLM and MCL Admissions Administrator | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs LLM and MCL Admissions Administrator Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs LLM and MCL Admissions Administrator The Faculty of Law is seeking to appoint an LLM and MCL Admissions Administrator to join our Professional Services Team. This role provides an exciting opportunity to be part of a supportive Faculty, proactive in providing training and career development opportunities for all staff. The Faculty of Law currently has over 100 University, Faculty and College Teaching Officers. Almost every area of legal interest is represented. Within its professional services team, the Faculty has 25 administrative staff, an IT team of 6. The role holder will provide support to the LLM and MCL Programmes Coordinator for admissions activity within the Faculty in order to provide an efficient admissions experience for prospective students.  The role holder will also provide general administrative support to the LLM and MCL Programmes Coordinator (for those two taught postgraduate programmes). The role holder will provide appropriate support for work undertaken on student information systems i.e. Panel and CamSIS, support circulation of applications to the two degree programmes, support Faculty events held for LLM and MCL students and produce databases and tables of information relating to the programmes. Applicants should have excellent interpersonal and communication skills and a good standard of written and spoken English. The role requires previous administrative experience and excellent IT skills. The role holder must be able to work on their own initiative, in addition to working as part of a team. We offer the flexibility of hybrid working after the probation period and would be willing to discuss this as part of the interview process. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment. If you have any questions about this vacancy please contact Suzanne Wade, the LLM and MCL Coordinator at shw27@cam.ac.uk . If you have questions about the application process, please contact the HR office in the Faculty at hr@law.cam.ac.uk The closing date for applications is 27 September 2026. Interviews will be held on 12 October 2026. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please quote reference JK50816 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Faculty of Law Salary £27,319-£31,236 Reference JK50816 Category Assistant staff Date published 27 August 2026 Closing date 27 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/mitigating-circumstances-coordinator-fixed-term-ab50436": {
      "title": "Mitigating Circumstances Coordinator (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Mitigating Circumstances Coordinator (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Mitigating Circumstances Coordinator (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Mitigating Circumstances Coordinator (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available until 31 July 2028 in the first instance. The Team The Exam Operations and Mitigating Circumstances team sit within the Student Administration section of Education Services, supporting the academic purposes of the University.  We provide expert administrative support and advice to the University and Colleges relating to the operational delivery of exams and reasonable adjustments for undergraduate and taught postgraduate courses. We also manage the administration of mitigating circumstances and allowances for undergraduate and taught postgraduate students. About the Role This is a rewarding opportunity to work within an established, supportive team and will be for two years fixed term. The existing Coordinator will be seconded on to an exciting project within the wider exam team. The role is responsible for delivering the administration of examination allowances and adjustments for students by ensuring that applications for examination access arrangements (reasonable adjustments), including adjusted modes of assessment and supporting the examination allowance process for postgraduate students are processed. Given the role requirements, we invite applications from individuals who: have excellent interpersonal and communication skills; have the ability to work collaboratively as well as independently; are positive, approachable and have a diplomatic manner with a wide range of stakeholders; able to foster positive working relationships; are conscientious, will contribute to the continuing success of the team; are motivated and takes initiative; are highly meticulous, accurate and organised; are highly flexible and pro-active in approach; are quick and comfortable to adapt to change; have strong organisational skills and multi-tasking ability. Given the demands of the examination cycle, the role requires a flexible approach to working patterns and the ability to adjust priorities when needed.  Whilst the Exam Team is supportive of hybrid working, there are periods of time where in office working is non-negotiable.  The role holder will be office based during their initial 6-month probation period and for the exam round from April-June.  During this time there will also be some Saturday, evening and Bank Holiday working, for which time off in lieu will be granted. The Exam Team forms part of the Student Administration Service within Education Services and exceptionally, you may be required to work on other tasks within Education Services if there is a change in priorities. Benefits of working at the University of Cambridge include: An opportunity to be part of a large, experienced, supportive and collaborative team; Competitive rates of pay with automatic service-related pay progression and annual cost of living increases; Excellent pension scheme; Generous annual leave allowance Applications are welcome  from both external and internal candidates, including internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Additional information regarding the role can be found within the Further Particulars. When applying, please upload your CV and cover letter which clearly sets out how you meet the criteria listed in the person specification of the Further Particulars (the knowledge, skills, and experience required for the role) along with relevant examples. Candidates will be shortlisted and invited to interview based upon these criteria. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be initially directed by email to sscjobs@admin.cam.ac.uk The closing date for applications is Wednesday 16 September 2026 The expected, in-person, interviews will be held on Monday 05 November 2026 Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you have any queries regarding the application process please contact SSCjobs@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference AB50436 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Mitigating Circumstances Coordinator FPs Key information Department/location Student Administration Salary £33,951-£39,906 Reference AB50436 Category Academic-related Date published 4 September 2026 Closing date 16 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/mphil-in-precision-medical-imaging-ai-course-premia-coordinator-fixed-term-rq50799": {
      "title": "MPhil in Precision Medical Imaging & AI Course (PREMIA) Coordinator (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "MPhil in Precision Medical Imaging & AI Course (PREMIA) Coordinator (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs MPhil in Precision Medical Imaging & AI Course (PREMIA) Coordinator (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs MPhil in Precision Medical Imaging & AI Course (PREMIA) Coordinator (Fixed Term) The Department of Radiology is seeking an organised and proactive Course Administrator to support the delivery of its new taught MPhil in Precision Medical Imaging and AI (PREMIA), which will welcome its first cohort of students in October 2027. This is an exciting opportunity to play a central role in establishing and supporting an innovative postgraduate programme operating at the interface of cutting-edge research, advanced imaging technology and clinical practice. You will help ensure the smooth running of the programme's teaching and learning activities, providing high-quality administrative support throughout the student lifecycle. Working closely with students, academics, clinicians and technical staff, you will coordinate a broad range of activities, which may include academic timetabling, meetings and committees, coursework and assessment processes, examinations, student communications and programme events. You will also support financial administration, maintain accurate records, collate and analyse data for reporting purposes, and assist with monitoring programme activities and expenditure.\nThe role is based within the Department of Radiology but involves close collaboration with colleagues across the Cambridge Biomedical Campus, including Cambridge University Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust. It therefore offers a varied working environment in which effective communication, teamwork and the ability to build positive working relationships will be essential. The Department of Radiology combines scientific excellence with access to some of the UK's most advanced imaging facilities. Equipped with state-of-the-art clinical and preclinical MRI, PET and CT imaging equipment, our comprehensive research ecosystem enables world-class investigation into disease mechanisms, accelerates the development of new diagnostic and therapeutic approaches, and provides an unparalleled training environment for the next generation of imaging scientists.\nThe successful candidate will be educated to HND/HNC or Level 4/5 standard, or have an equivalent level of practical experience. They will have proven experience in a comprehensive administrative or coordination role, ideally within an academic, higher education, clinical or healthcare environment. You will be highly organised, methodical and accurate, with the ability to manage competing priorities, meet deadlines and work independently with minimal supervision. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, strong IT proficiency and a confident approach to working with students and senior colleagues are essential. Experience of postgraduate course administration, committee support, financial processes, student record systems or academic timetabling would be advantageous. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 5 years in the first instance. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a health assessment. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role on the online application form.\nPlease include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Closing date: 30th September 2026 Interview Date: To be Confirmed Please quote reference RQ50799 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Radiology Salary £31,236-£35,608 Reference RQ50799 Category Assistant staff Date published 20 August 2026 Closing date 30 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/mrc-bsu-phd-studentship-sl49643-0": {
      "title": "Breadcrumb",
      "description": "MRC BSU PhD Studentship | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs MRC BSU PhD Studentship Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs MRC BSU PhD Studentship The Medical Research Council (MRC) Biostatistics Unit (BSU) is one of the largest groups of biostatisticians in Europe, and a major centre for research, training and knowledge transfer in biostatistics. We develop, apply and promote innovative statistical and data science approaches to advance biomedical science and human health.  The BSU current research portfolio is organised into five main themes that span the scientific research spectrum from basic science to population health and respond to current scientific needs in biomedicine. We welcome applications from students who wish to apply innovative statistical methods to real biomedical problems in order to deliver key insights into human health and disease. One highly competitive fully funded (covering University fees and stipend) three-year studentship will be available to a UK applicant from January 2027. Our departmental funding includes a generous travel and training budget. The Unit is actively seeking to increase diversity among its doctoral students, including promoting an equitable representation of men and women. The Unit therefore especially encourages applications from under-represented groups, including women and minority ethnic groups. For further information regarding eligibility and PhD projects available please see PhD Programme MRC Biostatistics Unit ( https://www.mrc-bsu.cam.ac.uk/phd-programme ) To apply please see the University's postgraduate application portal here: Postgraduate Study University of Cambridge ( https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/ ) Application closing date: 21st September 2026. Interviews will be held shortly thereafter 2026. For further enquiries please email: phdstudy@mrc-bsu.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference SL49643 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Key information Department/location MRC Biostatistics Unit Salary Reference SL49643 Category Studentships Date published 7 May 2026 Closing date 21 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/natural-history-engagement-coordinator-fixed-term-pe50868": {
      "title": "Natural History Engagement Coordinator (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Natural History Engagement Coordinator (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Natural History Engagement Coordinator (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Natural History Engagement Coordinator (Fixed Term) We are looking for an enthusiastic and well-connected Natural History Engagement Coordinator to join Collections-Connections-Communities (CCC), a Strategic Research Initiative at the University of Cambridge which empowers researchers to use the University's collections to address the big questions of our time. Cambridge holds one of the world's most significant natural history collections internationally important zoological, botanical, geological and palaeontological specimens and living collections, alongside related archives, rare books and artworks. This is a fixed-term post within a new Collections and Participation team, funded for one year in the first instance, with potential extension to July 2030, working alongside a Programmes Coordinator and two Research Associates. You will coordinate a varied programme of engagement and training activities based on these collections and the research around them, broadening access for external audiences, communities and partners. You will co-design and deliver training and learning programmes, workshops, seminars and resources that build capacity, working with natural history collections, biodiversity data and living collections. You will build relationships with museums, botanic gardens, herbaria, conservation organisations, libraries and archives, coordinate networks and communities of practice connecting collection professionals, researchers, educators and policymakers, and support collaborative projects that ensure our collections-based research will impact on the challenges of biodiversity, environmental change and sustainability. We're looking for someone with experience of developing, coordinating or delivering externally focused programmes, events, workshops or training activities, and of working with natural history collections, museums, botanic gardens, herbaria or biodiversity organisations. An understanding of the role of these collections in research, conservation, education and innovation, and of public engagement and knowledge exchange in a research or cultural environment, is essential, as are excellent communication and networking skills, strong project coordination skills, and a degree or equivalent professional experience in biodiversity, natural sciences or a natural history discipline. Knowledge of other collection domains, familiarity with digitisation or biodiversity informatics, experience of designing learning programmes for a range of ages, and a postgraduate or teaching qualification would be an advantage. This is a chance to work across a collections ecosystem of exceptional depth, where the most interesting opportunities often emerge in the connections between a specimen, a living plant, an archive and a rare book. The networks you build will help maximise the value of collections that speak directly to biodiversity loss and environmental change. Occasional evening and weekend working, and occasional travel within the UK and internationally, may be required. If you'd like to connect one of the world's great natural history collections with the people and organisations who need it, we'd love to hear from you. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 1 years in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For any questions relating to this recruitment please contact admin@botanic.cam.ac.uk The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Role Profile/Further information Key information Department/location Botanic Garden Salary £33,951-£39,906 Reference PE50868 Category Academic-related Date published 27 August 2026 Closing date 11 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/next-generation-fellow-fixed-term-pm50284": {
      "title": "Next Generation Fellow (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Next Generation Fellow (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Next Generation Fellow (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Next Generation Fellow (Fixed Term) The Loke Centre for Trophoblast Research (CTR) was founded in 2007 as a Centre of excellence to promote scientific study of the placenta, early development and maternal-fetal interactions during pregnancy. It brings together over 30 Principal Investigators, many of whom are recognized international experts in their fields. The Loke CTR has an opening for a Next Generation Fellow who will complement and synergise with our existing research programmes. Next Generation Fellowships are a springboard to a long and successful career in the field of placental and reproductive biology and highly flexible to suit individual needs, to enable the postholder to develop as an independent researcher. Holders are expected, and will be supported, to apply for Research Fellowship/Career Development Awards from research councils or other funders, or a faculty position during their tenure. Find out more about the fellowship on our website: https://www.trophoblast.cam.ac.uk/ngf-overview The Next Generation Fellowships are a springboard to a long and successful career in the field of placental and reproductive biology. Many of our Next Generation Fellows have used the opportunity as a stepping stone to independent group leader positions: https://www.trophoblast.cam.ac.uk/next-generation-fellow-alumni . What to expect Next Generation Fellows will be mentored to develop and establish an independent research program that will complement that of other groups within the Centre for Trophoblast Research. Fellows will be expected to contribute fully to the activities of the Loke CTR and to their host department. They will supervise MPhil/masters, summer and/or undergraduate students. They may be a co-applicant on research grant applications to recruit, train and co-supervise postdoctoral researchers, research assistants, technical staff and PhD students. The post holder will be expected to create a strong track record of publishing high impact papers and disseminate the results of their work in any ways that seem appropriate and effective (e.g. via conferences and seminars). They will be responsible for compliance with all the relevant laws (Home Office, HTA, HFEA). Candidates should hold a PhD and have a high-quality publication track record in academic journals or equivalent.  We welcome applications from across a diverse range of research relevant to our Grand Challenges https://www.trophoblast.cam.ac.uk/about-us/objectives . Contract details Appointments will be made on a fixed-term, full-time, basis for a period of 3 years (Grade 7, £37,694- £46,049 for appointments in 2027). There is a possibility of up to 1-year extension. The contract further includes research consumables/equipment of £60,000 and travel support up to £750 over the duration of the fellowship. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. How to apply To apply to this post, candidates should first identify a research Sponsor from the list of Principal Investigators https://www.trophoblast.cam.ac.uk/people/principal-investigators within the Centre for Trophoblast Research, and discuss their research plans with them. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. In addition to this, please ensure that you upload the application documentations as follows: Curriculum Vitae (CV) Up to 3 letters of reference Letter of support from your chosen Loke CTR sponsor Loke CTR Next Generation Fellowship application form Closing date: midnight on 31st January 2027. Please quote reference PM50284 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Application Form Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Physiology, Development and Neuroscience Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference PM50284 Category Research Date published 15 July 2026 Closing date 31 January 2027 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/occupational-health-clinic-nurse-as50562": {
      "title": "Breadcrumb",
      "description": "Occupational Health Clinic Nurse | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Occupational Health Clinic Nurse Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Occupational Health Clinic Nurse An exciting opportunity has arisen for a clinic nurse to join our small but busy team. This is a full-time post, but part-time hours will be considered. The Occupational Health Service provides a compliance-based service to all University staff and postgraduate students, and some undergraduate student groups and its broad remit is to prevent work-associated ill health and to promote health at work. In doing so it works alongside other University central services such as the Safety Office. The role includes the screening of professionally regulated students i.e., Medical, Veterinary and PGCE (teacher) students, and staff undertaking research in the NHS, routine health surveillance, work related immunisations and health promotion. Reporting to the Senior Occupational Health Nurse Adviser you will carry out immunisation, phlebotomy and health screening to staff and some student groups. You will be expected to manage your own clinics, have excellent organisational skills and the self-confidence to work independently, with access to clinical advice as needed. Effective communication and team working skills together with confident use of standard IT systems including Microsoft Office are essential. Applicants must be registered with the Nursing and Midwifery Council (NMC) within the adult branch. Please see the Further Particulars link for a full job description, person specification and more information relating to this post. Interviews will be scheduled for the week commencing 12th October 2026. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a Disclosure and Barring Service check (enhanced with children and adults' barred lists) and a health assessment. This appointment also requires NMC Registration. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to Alana Chatterley at: OccHealth@admin.cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference AS50562 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Occupational Health and Safety Service Salary £31,236-£35,608 Reference AS50562 Category Assistant staff Date published 2 September 2026 Closing date 30 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/pa-and-office-administrator-fixed-term-go50749": {
      "title": "Breadcrumb",
      "description": "PA and Office Administrator (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs PA and Office Administrator (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs PA and Office Administrator (Fixed Term) Applications are invited for the role of PA and Office Administrator (Grade 5) at the Centre for the Study of Existential Risk (CSER). An exciting opportunity has arisen within the core administrative team, supporting the work of CSER, and joining a growing core team across the Institute for Technology and Humanity (ITH). This is a full-time, fixed-term post where funding is available for two years in the first instance. The expected start date for this role is Monday 2 November 2026, or as soon as possible thereafter. Due to the nature of this role, the role holder will be expected to work from the CSER office Monday to Friday, with some flexibility to work from home during quieter periods, to be agreed with the line manager. Reporting to the Centre Co-ordinator, the role will largely focus on PA support for the Director and other members of the senior leadership team, office management, and managing an extensive visitor and affiliate programme, as well as organising internal seminars and events, and other activities to support the day-to-day running of CSER. This is a diverse and dynamic position, and the role holder may be asked to undertake other duties as appropriate. The post holder is expected to take a proactive and flexible approach to their work and will have relevant administrative experience and excellent organisational skills. More information about the Centre for the Study of Existential Risk can be found here: https://www.cser.ac.uk Further details about this role can be found in the 'Further Information' document located in the top left-hand corner of this advertisement. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 2 years in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries about the role are welcomed and can be directed to recruitment@cser.cam.ac.uk For questions on the application process, please contact the School's HR Team on sahhr@admin.cam.ac.uk The closing date for applications is midnight (BST) on Sunday 20 September 2026. Interviews are planned to take place in October 2026, subject to change. Please quote reference GO50749 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Cambridge Institute for Technology and Humanity Salary £31,236-£33,002 Reference GO50749 Category Assistant staff Date published 14 August 2026 Closing date 20 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/performance-assistant-dj50892": {
      "title": "Performance Assistant",
      "description": "Performance Assistant | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Performance Assistant Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Performance Assistant The University's Centre for Music Performance (CMP) is seeking a Performance Assistant to join our dynamic and supportive team. About the division/school The CMP is an umbrella organisation which enables students of all subjects, backgrounds and levels of musical ability to make extracurricular music - in all its forms - a core part of life here, thereby enriching the holistic educational experience on offer at Cambridge. It is dedicated to strengthening further opportunities on offer for the university's most able performers, developing a new programme of entry level opportunities for musical beginners, and enhancing support for the thriving ecosystem of student-led music-making. While maintaining the university's core strengths in Western classical music, it is also extending support to a significantly wider range of musical genres. The Role As Performance Assistant, you will provide essential support towards the smooth running of our music programme, supporting ensembles, concerts, performance workshops, masterclasses and other projects. This position is an exciting opportunity for someone to gain experience in arts management, learning from industry professionals and working with guest artists. It will suit someone who enjoys a busy and demanding workload, able to work on multiple projects simultaneously, and has a passion for music-making. Please note that working hours will be flexible around delivering events and activity, including during evenings and weekends, for which Time Off in Lieu will be provided. About you Interested applicants for this post should be educated to GCSE level grades A-C or equivalent/NVQ Level 2 or equivalent level of practical experience and have excellent IT skills. Your application should clearly set out how you meet the Person Profile criteria listed in the Further Particulars (see below) along with relevant examples. Candidates will be shortlisted based upon these criteria. Once an offer of employment has been made the successful candidate will be required to complete a work health declaration. Benefits of working at Cambridge University include: Competitive rates of pay with automatic service-related pay progression and annual cost of living increases. Generous annual leave allowance of 36 days paid leave (including bank holidays). For part-time employees, annual leave will be pro rata'd based on days worked. Generous maternity, adoption and shared parental leave entitlement and other family friendly schemes (e.g. workplace nurseries and salary exchange schemes for childcare). An auto-enrolment pension scheme, with a generous employer contribution. Travel benefits and retail discounts at over 2,000 local and national stores. Schemes to support with relocation/the provision of accommodation. Interviews will be held on Thursday 17 September 2026. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries about the role should be directed to: Chloe Davidson, Centre for Music Performance Manager, email: cnd26@cam.ac.uk If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact Karin Kuchta, HR Coordinator, email: csihhr@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference DJ50892 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information DJ50892 - Further Particulars Key information Department/location Centre for Music Performance Salary £25,249-£27,319 Reference DJ50892 Category Assistant staff Date published 28 August 2026 Closing date 13 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/post-doctoral-research-associate-indoor-air-quality-fixed-term-ma50851": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Research Associate: Indoor air quality (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Post-Doctoral Research Associate: Indoor air quality (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Post-Doctoral Research Associate: Indoor air quality (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Post-Doctoral Research Associate: Indoor air quality (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 1 year in the first instance. Applications are invited for a post-doctoral research associate to join the group of Professor Chiara Giorio at the Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry to work on indoor air quality in homes within the framework of the INQUIRE project, a EU-Horizon Europe-funded research and innovation action involving 20 institutions across Europe and Australia. INQUIRE investigates the chemical and biological determinants of indoor air quality (IAQ) in over 200 European homes with infants and young children, with the aim of protecting citizen health and informing future IAQ regulation. You will lead UCAM's activities within Task 5.2 (\"Associations between IAQ, Internal Exposure and Human Health\"), working alongside co-leads at other European institutes and other WP1¿WP4 partners generating the underlying air quality, biomonitoring, and toxicity data. Key responsibilities: Lead UCAM's contribution to Task 5.2, including modelling of respiratory deposition of indoor particulate matter and estimation of exposure to aerosol-bound chemicals, using measurement data generated in WP2 (chemical/biological profiling) and WP3 (toxicity assessment). Apply lung deposition modelling approaches (accounting for age, gender, activity level, and respiratory tract disease status) to translate ambient/indoor concentration data into internal dose estimates. Contribute to statistical analyses linking IAQ and human biomonitoring (HBM) data to self-reported health outcomes and questionnaire-derived exposure determinants (e.g. socioeconomic status, building characteristics, product use). Collaborate with WP1 (sampling/biomonitoring), WP2 (chemical/biological characterisation) and WP3 (toxicity) partners to ensure appropriate integration of exposure and effect data. Contribute findings to INQUIRE's cross-project synthesis and to the \"INQUIRE Action Plan for Promoting Healthy Homes in Europe\". Prepare data for deposition in FAIR repositories and co-author scientific publications and stakeholder-facing outputs. Contribute to INQUIRE reporting requirements. Present results at INQUIRE consortium meetings (General Assembly, WP5 workshops) and relevant international conferences. Contribute to supervision of any associated students/research assistants at UCAM working on related tasks. Essential requirements: PhD (or near completion) in environmental science, exposure science, aerosol/atmospheric science, epidemiology, biostatistics, or a closely related field. Strong quantitative/statistical skills and experience handling large, multi-source environmental and/or biomonitoring datasets. Proficiency in a relevant programming/statistical environment (e.g. R, Python, or MATLAB). Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to work effectively within a large, multidisciplinary, multinational consortium. Willingness to travel occasionally within Europe for consortium meetings. Desirable: Demonstrable experience in exposure modelling and/or air pollution health-effects research (e.g. particle deposition modelling, dosimetry, or exposure¿response analysis). Experience with human biomonitoring data or epidemiological cohort data (e.g. birth cohort studies). Familiarity with indoor air quality sensor data and/or suspect/non-target chemical screening datasets. Experience with FAIR data principles and open-science data management. Prior experience within EU Horizon/H2020-funded consortium projects. Anticipated Start Date: as soon as possible after recruitment process is completed (e.g., September/October 2026) We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload your Curriculum Vitae (CV), a covering letter and publications list in the upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents that have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. For queries relating to your application or the application process, please contact Prof Chiara Giorio via email on cg525@cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference MA50851 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The Department holds an Athena SWAN silver award for women in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics, and Medicine. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further information Key information Department/location Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry Salary £34,610  £35,608 £37,694  £46,049 Reference MA50851 Category Research Date published 25 August 2026 Closing date 15 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/postdoctoral-research-associate-evaluating-food-marketing-restrictions-fixed-term-sj50788": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (evaluating food marketing restrictions) [Fixed Term]",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (evaluating food marketing restrictions) [Fixed Term] | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate (evaluating food marketing restrictions) [Fixed Term] Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate (evaluating food marketing restrictions) [Fixed Term] Postdoctoral Research Associate evaluating food marketing restrictions, Population Health Interventions Group, Institute of Metabolic Science (IMS) Epidemiology IMS Epidemiology is seeking to appoint a Postdoctoral Research Associate with skills and experience in quantitative methods for evaluation of natural experiments to contribute to a multi-component evaluation of new restrictions on marketing of less healthy foods. New restrictions on advertising of less healthy food and drinks online and on TV before 9pm were implemented in the UK in January 2026. Restrictions on price-based promotions of less healthy products in England were implemented in October 2025. Based in the Population Health Interventions Group, the primary role of this post is to lead quantitative analyses exploring the impact of the new restrictions on: 1. household grocery purchasing, using data we will purchase from Worldpanel by Numerator; and 2. nutritional content of foods served by chain restaurants, using data from our MenuTracker database. We anticipate that both pieces of work will use of interrupted time series analysis methods. Funded by the National Institute of Health Research (NIHR), we are conducting a multi-component evaluation of the restrictions. Across the whole evaluation, we will study impacts on marketing, availability and purchasing of less healthy products; model impacts on health and wider society; explore awareness and acceptability; and interrogate policy processes. The work is led from IMS Epidemiology and involves colleagues at the Universities of Oxford, Stirling, Exeter, Waterloo and the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine. You will be educated to PhD level in a relevant subject or due to complete within 3 months of applying. You will have proven skills and experience in quantitative methods for evaluation of natural experiments, such as interrupted time series or difference-in-difference analysis. A complete list of essential and desirable criteria for this post is provided in the further particulars. Your application is unlikely to be competitive if it does not provide clear and concise evidence that you meet all of the essential criteria. Alongside a competitive salary, University staff are eligible for a comprehensive package of benefits and services including discounts on shopping, private health care, financial services and public transport. We also offer defined benefits pension schemes and tax-efficient bicycle, car lease and charity-giving schemes. This is a fixed-term position for 21 months. The role can be performed as a hybrid position, recognising that some tasks may only be performed on University premises. Please contact Professor Jean Adams, jma79@medschl.cam.ac.uk for informal enquiries. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Applicants must have (due to complete within 3 months of applying) a PhD. Appointment at Research Associate level is dependent on having a PhD. Those who have submitted but not yet received their PhD will initially be appointed as a Research Assistant (Grade 5) moving to Research Associate (Grade 7) upon confirmation of your PhD award. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Closing Date: 14th September 2026 Interview Date: 29th or 30th September 2026 Please quote reference SJ50788 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Clinical Biochemistry (Institute of Metabolic Science Epidemiology) Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference SJ50788 Category Research Date published 19 August 2026 Closing date 14 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
