    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistantassociate-in-systems-microbiology-fixed-term-nm50835": {
      "title": "Research Assistant/Associate in Systems Microbiology (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant/Associate in Systems Microbiology (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant/Associate in Systems Microbiology (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant/Associate in Systems Microbiology (Fixed Term) We are seeking a creative and highly motivated Research Assistant/Associate to join Dr Somenath Bakshi's research group in the Department of Engineering. The project will investigate how bacterial physiological state shapes bacteriophage infection. We aim to determine how dormancy, nutrient limitation, growth transitions and stress generate transient phage resistance or tolerance, identify the underlying mechanisms, and distinguish cells that prevent productive infection from those that become infected but survive or delay killing. The post is based in Central Cambridge, UK. The successful applicant will combine bacterial genetics, genetic engineering and experimental phage biology to perturb host physiology and test causal mechanisms. The work will include constructing bacterial strains and reporters, establishing phage-host systems, performing quantitative infection assays under defined physiological conditions, and using fluorescence or time-lapse microscopy to link pre-infection state to infection outcome. The post holder will work with the Bakshi Lab's single-cell phage infection platform ( https://www.bakshilab.net/ ) and an interdisciplinary team spanning microbiology, engineering and quantitative modelling. Applicants should have obtained, or be close to obtaining, a PhD in Microbiology, Molecular Microbiology, Phage Biology, Synthetic Biology, Biotechnology, Bioengineering, or a related discipline. Strong hands-on experience in bacterial microbiology, genetic engineering and bacteriophage methods is essential, together with some exposure to microscopy and quantitative data analysis. Experience with dormancy, persistence or stress physiology, bacterial defence systems, single-cell imaging, microfluidics, image analysis, Python or R would be advantageous. Appointment at Research Associate level is dependent on having a PhD. Those who have submitted but not yet received their PhD will be appointed at Research Assistant level, which will be amended to Research Associate once the PhD has been awarded. Salary Ranges: Research Assistant: £33,002 - £35,608 Research Associate: £37,694 - £46,049 Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 24 months in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload your Curriculum Vitae (CV) and a covering letter [add up to 3 further documents needed e.g. research publication list] in the Upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please submit your application by midnight on the closing date. If you have any questions about this vacancy, please contact Liz Elias (Group Administrator) via email at control-admin@eng.cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference NM50835 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information HR7_FurtherParticulars Key information Department/location Department of Engineering Salary £34,610  £35,608 £37,694  £46,049 Reference NM50835 Category Research Date published 24 August 2026 Closing date 21 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistantassociate-in-the-physics-of-radio-pulsars-fixed-term-ka50760": {
      "title": "Research Assistant/Associate in the Physics of Radio Pulsars (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant/Associate in the Physics of Radio Pulsars (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant/Associate in the Physics of Radio Pulsars (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant/Associate in the Physics of Radio Pulsars (Fixed Term) We are seeking an enthusiastic and motivated Research Assistant/Associate to work on the ERC funded project PulsarMAP: \"Pulsar Mode-changing And Polarization: mapping out a connected picture of neutron star evolution\". This project will advance our fundamental understanding of the physics of radio pulsars and the interstellar medium by bringing together studies of the time-variability and polarization properties of large-scale radio pulsar data sets. The successful candidate will join a group dedicated to PulsarMAP, working with Dr Lucy Oswald, and collaborating with an international team on the Thousand-Pulsar-Array project, which studies the pulsar population using the exquisite sensitivity of the MeerKAT radio telescope. You will contribute to project goals in one or more of the following areas: studying how polarized pulsar radio emission is scattered by structures in the interstellar medium, mapping the impact of mode-changing and other short-term time-variability on the pulsar population, and linking polarization and time-variability physics to understand pulsar magnetospheres. Essential skills include strong experience of programming in Python, excellent communication skills, the ability to handle large data sets with ease, and an aptitude for relating theoretical models to observational features. The role holder must have experience working with radio pulsar data, ideally including experience with one or more of the following: single-pulse studies (such as subpulse drifting, nulling or mode-changing), interstellar medium studies with pulsars, or studies of radio pulsar polarization. Appointment at Research Associate level is dependent upon having a PhD. Those who have submitted but not yet received their PhD will be appointed at Research Assistant level, which will be uplifted to Research Associate once the PhD has been awarded.\nThe role is available for two years in the first instance, with the possibility of extension. We encourage early applications as they will be reviewed on a rolling basis. The vacancy may close before the advertised deadline if a sufficient number of suitable applications are received. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 2 years in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Candidates should include a CV, covering letter and details of two referees. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to Dr Lucy Oswald at lo280@cam.ac.uk Please quote reference KA50760 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information KA50760 further particulars Key information Department/location Department of Physics Salary £33,002-£46,049 Reference KA50760 Category Research Date published 14 August 2026 Closing date 27 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
