    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistantresearch-associate-ai-tutors-for-stem-education-fixed-term-nr50921": {
      "title": "Research Assistant/Research Associate",
      "description": "Research Assistant/Research Associate - AI Tutors for STEM Education (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant/Research Associate - AI Tutors for STEM Education (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant/Research Associate - AI Tutors for STEM Education (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 2 years. We are seeking to appoint a full-time Post-Doctoral Research Associate (PDRA) to lead the design, evaluation and deployment of an AI-powered tutor within the Isaac Science and Ada Computer Science online learning platforms.\nThis is an exciting position within a well-established and successful online STEM education project and a chance to make an immediate real-world impact with your work in this fast-developing and exciting new area in the education sector. Isaac Science, developed by the University of Cambridge, and Ada Computer Science, developed in collaboration with the Raspberry Pi Foundation, use modern web technologies and evidence-based educational approaches to improve STEM education. The platform provides online learning resources to improve physics, mathematics, chemistry, biology and computer science teaching to students aged 11-18 and is used extensively by schools across the UK and internationally. The platform currently supports hundreds of thousands of students and teachers and receives millions of question attempts each month. The successful candidate will lead a major new initiative to develop a Socratic AI tutor capable of providing high quality, pedagogically informed support to learners as they work through questions on the platform. The AI tutor will be designed to guide student thinking through hints, prompts and explanations while deliberately avoiding the disclosure of complete answers. The objective is to enhance learning, develop student understanding and strengthen problem solving approaches. The work will include a review of existing AI tutoring and AI-assisted learning systems, including commercial products and research prototypes. The successful candidate will work alongside our dedicated software engineering team to design and implement a prototype AI tutor as well as collaborate with our content and education teams where they will undertake trials with partner schools, evaluate learning outcomes and user experiences, and contribute to the deployment of successful approaches at scale across Isaac Science and Ada Computer Science. Research Environment The PDRA will be based within the Department of Computer Science and Technology at the University of Cambridge, a world-leading centre for research and innovation in computer science, education technology and computing education. The role will involve close collaboration with: The Isaac Science and Ada Computer Science software engineering team The Isaac Science content and education team Colleagues at the Raspberry Pi Foundation developing content for Ada Computer Science Subject matter experts and content developers across STEM disciplines Researchers within the Raspberry Pi Computing Education Research Centre Academic researchers in AI, learning sciences and STEM education Teachers and schools participating in platform evaluation and trials Members of the wider UK and international community exploring the responsible use of AI in schools The successful candidate will have a unique opportunity to influence the future of AI-enabled learning while working within an established and highly successful educational project in a world-class computer science department. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Key Responsibilities Conduct research into AI tutoring systems, generative AI, educational AI and related literature. Evaluate existing commercial and research AI tutoring products and identify best practice. Design frameworks for pedagogically effective AI-generated feedback and hints. Lead the design of an AI tutor for Isaac Science and Ada Computer Science. Work closely with software engineers to implement and test prototype systems. Develop approaches to ensure AI-generated responses support learning without directly providing answers. Design and conduct evaluations, pilot studies and school-based trials. Analyse quantitative and qualitative evidence from platform usage and educational studies. Disseminate findings through academic publications, conferences, reports and stakeholder engagement. Contribute to grant proposals and future research initiatives where appropriate. Collaborate effectively with academic, technical and educational partners. Contribute to the wider research activities of the Department. A complete list of requirements and desirable criteria for the role may be found in the further particulars document.  Examples of the essential criteria for this role include: A PhD (or be close to completion) in computer science, artificial intelligence, machine learning, human-computer interaction, education, learning sciences, educational technology, computing education or another directly relevant discipline. Experience of educational technology research or development. Experience designing or evaluating digital learning interventions What we offer Conduct impactful research in one of the most significant emerging areas of educational technology. Shape how AI is used to support learning in schools. Work with a highly experienced team of researchers, software engineers and subject experts. Collaborate with leading organisations, including the Raspberry Pi Foundation. Access established networks of partner schools ready to participate in pilot studies and evaluations. Publish impactful research while contributing to a widely used educational platform. Be part of a successful, long-running University of Cambridge project with a strong record of innovation and impact. Contribute to teaching and mentoring activities within the Department where appropriate. In addition, the University offers an excellent working environment, generous annual leave entitlement, membership of the USS pension scheme, extensive professional development opportunities and flexible working arrangements. Appointment at the Research Associate level is dependent on the award of a PhD. This is a postdoctoral-level appointment, those who have submitted but not yet received their PhD will be appointed at the Research Assistant -Grade 5, which will be amended to Research Associate-Grade 7 once the award of the PhD has been conferred. Further information For more information about the platforms, please visit: https://isaacscience.org https://adacomputerscience.org Informal enquiries are welcome and may be directed to: Professor Alastair Beresford arb33@cam.ac.uk Applications should include a curriculum vitae, a covering letter describing relevant experience and interest in the role and contact details for two academic referees. Please quote reference NR50921 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. Apply online Download further information Further information Key information Department/location Department of Computer Science and Technology Salary £34,610  £35,608 £37,694  £46,049 Reference NR50921 Category Research Date published 2 September 2026 Closing date 16 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-fixed-term-pm50266": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) We seek a highly motivated Postdoctoral Research Associate to join the laboratory of Professor Kathy Niakan. We are based in the Loke Centre for Trophoblast Research (LCTR), in the Department of Physiology, Development and Neuroscience. A successful candidate will help to develop pioneering research projects to provide fundamental insights into human biology and inform novel strategies for stem cell biology and post-implantation development of human embryos. The aims of research projects in the lab are to characterise early lineage specification in human pre- and early post-implantation embryos. We seek to explore a range of methods to establish robust and reproducible cutting-edge single cell molecular analysis, imaging, functional genomics techniques and stem cell models to understand early lineage specification mechanisms in human embryos. Our laboratory aims to utilise the knowledge gained from these studies to provide fundamental insights into human biology. We also aim to use this knowledge to facilitate the development of robust and reproducible implantation models, novel human stem cells and stem cell-based models of human development. We perform comparative analysis of human embryogenesis to several other mammalian species, pluripotent stem cells and integrated stem cell models of human embryos. The successful candidate will be energetic, focused, and productive with a desire to work in a congenial, dynamic, and collaborative research environment. Excellent organizational, analytical, and communication skills are essential. Candidates should hold a PhD in a relevant subject. Please refer to the person specification for a full list of essential skills and qualifications. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The Department of Physiology, Development & Neuroscience particularly encourages women and candidates from a Black, Asian or Minority Ethnic background to apply for this vacancy. We will support visa application if assistance is needed. We have a legal responsibility to ensure that you have the right to work in the UK before you can start working for us. Any job application you submit to us will be assessed using criteria based on the knowledge, skills and experience required for the relevant post. You will not be treated less favourably than another applicant on the grounds of national origin. However, any offer of employment we make to you will be conditional upon you gaining permission to work in the UK. For further information: http://www.jobs.cam.ac.uk/right/have/ To find out more about the lab visit our website at: https://niakanlab.com/ To find out more about the LCTR please visit our website at: https://www.trophoblast.cam.ac.uk/ Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 3 years in the first instance. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check and a security check. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. To apply for this post, please submit a CV and a motivation letter detailing why you want to be part of this project and how this post will be a good stepping-stone for your career. We also need the contact details for two people who can provide a professional reference. Closing date: midnight on 13th September 2026. Please quote reference PM50266 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Physiology, Development and Neuroscience Salary £33,002-£46,049 Reference PM50266 Category Research Date published 3 August 2026 Closing date 13 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-fixed-term-pf50636": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) Applications are invited for a Postdoctoral Research Associate to join an exciting collaborative project, funded by the BBSRC, led by Matthias Landgraf in the Department of Zoology, University of Cambridge, ( https://www.zoo.cam.ac.uk/people/matthias-landgraf ) together with Marco Sciacovelli at the University of Liverpool ( https://www.liverpool.ac.uk/people/marco-sciacovelli ). The focus of the project are critical periods of nervous system development. These are windows of increased plasticity that enable neuronal networks to tune appropriately, based on sensory and environmental stimuli. However, when this process is perturbed, it leads to permanently maladjusted networks that affect behaviour and stability. In humans, sub-optimal critical period experiences are thought responsible for several neurodevelopmental conditions, including Schizophrenia, and forms of epilepsy. This project challenges our current understanding of the mechanistic underpinnings of critical period plasticity by proposing metabolic signals and metabolic reprogramming as central to this developmental process. The successful candidate will work with a powerful insect experimental model that we have developed: the locomotor network of the Drosophila embryo and larva. They will combine genetic and pharmacological approaches with imaging (of genetically encoded, fluorescent metabolite sensors), and integrate transcriptomics and metabolomics (targeted LC-MS and isotope tracing) data to analyse metabolic changes. Behavioural (seizure susceptibility and larval crawling) and anatomical observations (synaptic growth) will inform how metabolic reprogramming during the critical period leads to lasting phenotypic changes. We seek a new team member, based in the Department of Zoology in Cambridge, who is excited by neuroscience and how metabolism intersects with development. While primarily a \"wet-lab\" position, you should be comfortable with some bioinformatics analyses and data science of omics data. We value creativity, interdisciplinarity, curiosity, collaboration and inclusiveness. We offer a supportive, team-oriented research environment with opportunities for cutting-edge techniques and for addressing fundamental questions about how appropriate function emerges during late stages of nervous system development. For more information, please refer to the Further Particulars document Deadline for applications is midnight on 14 September 2026. Interviews are planned for the week commencing 5 October 2026 (subject to change). Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for up to 36 months (3 years). Preferred start date between November 2026 and January 2027. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to: Professor Matthias Landgraf [email: ml10006@cam.ac.uk ] If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact Zoology HR Office [email: hr@zoo.cam.ac.uk ] Please quote reference PF50636 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars - Research Assistant - PF50636 Key information Department/location Department of Zoology Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference PF50636 Category Research Date published 5 August 2026 Closing date 14 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-fixed-term-sm50861": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) The Medical Research Council Mitochondrial Biology Unit (MRC MBU), University of Cambridge wishes to appoint a post-doctoral Research Associate to join the research group of Prof. Michal Minczuk at the MRC Mitochondrial Biology Unit. \nA world-renowned centre for research, the MRC MBU is a vibrant community focused on the involvement of mitochondria and their dysfunction in an ever-increasing range of human diseases. Additional information can be found at www.mrc-mbu.cam.ac.uk The postholder will contribute to the Programmable mitochondria: development and validation of genetic transformation (ProMiT) multicentre project, funded through the ARIA Precision Mitochondria initiative, aimed at developing and rigorously testing new technologies for the delivery and functional use of genetic material in mitochondria. The role will support a shared experimental platform spanning complementary DNA- and RNA-focused approaches, using standardised cellular and biochemical systems and quantitative readouts. Working at the interface of mitochondrial biology, RNA biology, genome engineering and biochemistry, the successful candidate will establish mammalian cell models and mitochondrial preparations and develop assays for mitochondrial localisation and import, sub-mitochondrial distribution, membrane integrity and function. Approaches will include molecular biology, bioenergetics biochemical fractionation, sequencing, and single-cell approaches. The postholder will also contribute to mechanistic studies of mitochondrial RNA import in Protists, including development of robust in vitro assays. The role will also integrate molecular and functional datasets to support programme milestones and work closely with collaborators across the ARIA Precision Mitochondria programme. Candidates should hold, or be close to obtaining, a PhD in a relevant biological discipline and have strong experience in mitochondrial, cell, RNA or molecular biology, biochemistry, genetics or a related area. Experience in mammalian cell culture and genome engineering is important. Experience in mitochondrial isolation/fractionation, protease-protection assays, bioenergetics, sequencing or single-cell methods, flow cytometry, RNA biology or quantitative analysis would be highly advantageous. Informal enquiries about this position should be directed to Professor Michal Minczuk ( mam201@cam.ac.uk ). This is a fixed-term position for 36 months from the date of appointment, with the possibility of extension subject to programme progress and funding availability. The University of Cambridge is committed, in its pursuit of academic and research excellence, to a pro-active and inclusive approach to equality, which supports and encourages all under-represented groups, promotes an inclusive culture, and values diversity. The MBU is part of the School of Clinical Medicine, which holds an Athena Swan Silver Award and supports an Academic Women's Forum. The University particularly encourages women and candidates from Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic backgrounds to apply. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Appointment at Research Associate level is dependent on having a PhD. Those who have submitted but not yet received their PhD will initially be appointed as a Research Assistant (Grade 5) moving to Research Associate (Grade 7) upon confirmation of your PhD award. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Closing Date: 16th September 2026 Interview Date: 24th-25th  September 2026 Please quote reference SM50861 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location MRC Mitochondrial Biology Unit Salary £34,610  £35,608 £37,694  £46,049 Reference SM50861 Category Research Date published 26 August 2026 Closing date 16 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-fixed-term-ge50790": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) Applications are sought for three Research Associates to join the Cambridge Lab for Ancient World Studies (CLAWS), directed by Professor Josephine Quinn. Starting no later than 1 October 2027, you will anchor a new phase of activities for the Lab focusing on ancient entanglements in the broadest senses. As well as supporting CLAWS events and administration, you will conduct your own research project(s) within the broad themes of the Centre, addressing at least two different regions and/or periods and/or languages. One project will relate to semitic languages and cultures, broadly construed. A second will relate to archaeology, broadly construed, and cognate subjects. A third is open-field with respect to Ancient World Studies. Candidates may be considered for one, two, or all three positions. The appointments have been made possible with the support of the Faculties of Classics, Asian & Middle Eastern Studies, and History, as well as the McDonald Institute for Archaeological Research, and further details about the major co-funders may be found in the further particular document. Candidates will need to show evidence of the following qualifications, skills and experience: Doctorate in a relevant discipline (or clear evidence that the award of such a doctorate is imminent) Expertise in one or more areas of ancient world studies, including but not limited to semitic languages and cultures and/or archaeology and cognate subjects Relevant expertise and training to successfully carry out the proposed research project Willingness and ability to develop academic interests in new topics, regions, periods, and/or languages Record of relevant research publications (or evidence that such publications are in progress) commensurate with stage of career Ability to work as part of a team. In order for your application to be considered, please ensure you upload the following: Cover letter CV, including a list of publications Statement of max. 500 words in total summarizing (a) the significance of your current and existing research in relation to the aims and activities of CLAWS and (b) the research project you propose to undertake at Cambridge Full description of proposed research project (up to 1000 words, including bibliography) Sample(s) of academic work (up to 10,000 words with a preference for published work) Names and contact details of two academic referees who have agreed to provide references. These will be requested later in the process If you have any queries regarding the application process please contact the School HR Team on sahhr@admin.cam.ac.uk . The closing date for applications is midnight (BST) on Thursday 8 October 2026. Interviews are planned for Thursday 26 November 2026, subject to confirmation. The funds for this post are available for 2 years in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please quote reference GE50790 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Faculty of Classics Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference GE50790 Category Research Date published 27 August 2026 Closing date 8 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
