    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/teaching-professor-designated-principal-radiologist-pp50690": {
      "title": "Teaching Professor designated Principal Radiologist",
      "description": "Teaching Professor designated Principal Radiologist | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Teaching Professor designated Principal Radiologist Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Teaching Professor designated Principal Radiologist A leadership opportunity has arisen for an experienced specialist radiologist to advance their career by heading up the Diagnostic Imaging service team at the Queen's Veterinary School Hospital, Cambridge. We are a large, multidisciplinary small animal referral hospital, sitting within the University of Cambridge Department of Veterinary Medicine. The Hospital operates as a commercially competitive hospital, whilst remaining central to supporting teaching of veterinary undergraduate and postgraduate students, as well as delivering and supporting veterinary clinical and one health research. The QVSH also works collaboratively with our small animal and equine primary care practices and is supported by our in-house Clinical Pathology and Anatomical Pathology services. This role will be head of the Imaging service within the QVSH, responsible for leading, developing and supporting a high standard of clinical service provision in the Imaging team. The role holder will be responsible for ensuring this team works effectively as part of the wider referral hospital. Supporting clinical research is an important part of this role, as required for postgraduate training programmes, whilst balancing research impacts on the wider hospital requirement to provide a financially viable service. The department currently consists of 4 part-time Imaging Diplomates (in addition to this post), 3 ECVDI residents, 1 Imaging Intern and 5 radiographers. The service provides referral level MRI, CT, Ultrasound and Radiography to the QVSH both in and out of hours, as well as offering advice and support to our small animal and equine primary care practices. The role primarily provides a specialist diagnostic imaging clinical service while contributing to the clinical teaching of undergraduate (Year 6) veterinary students and postgraduate Clinical Training Scholars working within the QVSH. The postholder will support the development and delivery of undergraduate and postgraduate teaching and assessment in diagnostic imaging, in collaboration with academic colleagues. Although this is not primarily a teaching post, there will be opportunities to contribute to both clinical and didactic teaching. Time will be allocated for the administrative and service leadership components of the role. The role holder will be required to participate in an out-of-hours rota, for which an additional payment is provided. Applicants should be a Diplomate of the European College of Veterinary Diagnostic Imaging (Dip ECVDI) or American College of Veterinary Radiology (Dip ACVR) and be a member of the Royal College of Veterinary Surgeons (RCVS) or hold a veterinary degree registerable with the RCVS. Significant experience of working in a multidisciplinary veterinary referral hospital is essential along with in depth understanding of the operation of a commercial veterinary referral hospital. We offer a supportive and engaging environment with generous paid annual leave (41 days including the public holidays). In addition, we are able to provide competitive staff benefits and services, including family friendly policies, numerous discounts on shopping, health care, financial services, and public transport. We also offer a generous pension scheme, annual CPD allowance and tax-efficient bicycle, car lease and charity-giving schemes. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to Prof. Nick Bexfield, Hospital Director, by email: nb289@cam.ac.uk Further particulars for the role and information about the Department are available: https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/term/Department-of-Veterinary-Medicine If you are viewing this advert on an external site, please visit the University's job pages for access. Applications will be monitored regularly, and we may contact candidates prior to the closing date. We reserve the right to close this vacancy early if we receive a sufficient number of applications or extend the closing date if necessary. Therefore, if you are interested, please submit your application as early as possible. Please quote reference PP50690 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars - Teaching Professor designated Principal Radiologist Key information Department/location Department of Veterinary Medicine Salary £69,488-£80,525 plus Market Pay Supplement subject to approval Reference PP50690 Category Academic Date published 7 August 2026 Closing date 29 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/temporary-assistant-professor-in-modern-german-studies-gl50766": {
      "title": "Temporary Assistant Professor in Modern German Studies",
      "description": "Temporary Assistant Professor in Modern German Studies | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Temporary Assistant Professor in Modern German Studies Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Temporary Assistant Professor in Modern German Studies The German Section in the Faculty of Modern and Medieval Languages and Linguistics in the University of Cambridge is seeking to appoint an Assistant Professor in Modern German Studies, from 1 January 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. The post will provide temporary cover of the teaching and associated duties of a member of the Section during their tenure of a College headship and is fixed-term for 3 years. The Section will provide career development and mentoring for the successful candidate and is committed to equality, diversity and inclusion. Candidates will have relevant teaching experience, will have completed a PhD in a relevant field by the start date for the post, and be able to demonstrate active, collegial engagement in research, teaching and outreach in German Studies. An excellent command of German and English, spoken and written is essential. We seek a scholar with interdisciplinary expertise in modern literature, culture and history in the German-speaking countries. The successful applicant will be able to teach broadly and flexibly within the modern period; further, they will be able to contribute to the teaching of philosophy and intellectual history, and to teach translation between German and English. They will also have an outstanding record of research and publication, relative to their career stage, with clear potential for further excellence, and a proven capacity and readiness to contribute to administrative duties. We welcome applications from candidates who can contribute to the School of Arts and Humanities Global Humanities strategic research strand. For your application to be considered please submit the following: a covering letter a curriculum vita including a list of publications a sample of written work, research or scholarship and directly related to the field of the post, in English or German. This can be work that is published or work in progress, and it should be a single piece of work (as opposed to two shorter pieces). The maximum length is 8,000 words, including footnotes. You may redact parts of the sample if it is a longer piece, but the selection committee will stop reading at 8,000 words. the names and addresses of three referees who are familiar with your work. Please ask your referees to submit their references directly to references@mmll.cam.ac.uk by the closing date. Temporary cover: This post is fixed-term for 3 years or the return of the post holder, whichever is the earlier. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. The closing date for applications is midnight (BST) on Wednesday 16 September 2026. Interviews will be held on Wednesday 21 October 2026, subject to change. Prospective candidates are welcome to seek more information beyond the Further Particulars by contacting Dr Charlotte Lee, Director of German, at: cll38@cam.ac.uk . For questions on the application process, please contact the School's HR Team at: MMLLPersonnel@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference GL50766 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Information Key information Department/location Department of German and Dutch Salary £47,389-£59,966 Reference GL50766 Category Academic Date published 19 August 2026 Closing date 16 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/university-assistant-professor-in-polar-studies-lc49794": {
      "title": "University Assistant Professor in Polar Studies",
      "description": "University Assistant Professor in Polar Studies | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Skip to main content Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Research at Cambridge For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs University Assistant Professor in Polar Studies Jobs Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs University Assistant Professor in Polar Studies The Department of Geography and the Scott Polar Research Institute invite applications for a University Assistant Professorship in Polar Studies.  The post is available from 1 January 2027, or as soon as possible thereafter. The ideal candidate will bring expertise in Polar regions, with research and teaching interests in one or more of: terrestrial and freshwater environments, landscapes and biota, their responses to, and interactions with, other components of the Earth system, and their social/human dimensions. Candidates should hold a PhD in Geography, Polar Studies or related discipline and will have an outstanding academic record for their career stage. They should have a strong record of relevant research publications, evidence of research leadership (or potential to develop it) and the ability to attract research funding. They must show evidence of enthusiasm and ability to teach a wide range of courses in Geography and Polar Studies at both undergraduate and Masters level. They will have a growing international reputation and the ability to recruit and supervise high-level PhD and postdoctoral researchers. Applicants should submit the following documents with their application: Full Curriculum Vitae (CV). Full list of publications. Please highlight up to 5 papers where you have made the most significant personal contributions, with up to 4 lines of text for each such paper. A research statement (3 pages max.) outlining your interests and expertise, and describing how your research plans might complement and extend existing activities at the Scott Polar Research Institute, Department of Geography and the wider University. A teaching statement (2 pages max.) on your approach to teaching and how you believe you can contribute to the SPRI/Department's teaching programme at undergraduate, MPhil and PhD level. The contact details of three referees. More information about the role and the person specifications is available in the Further Particulars attached. We particularly welcome applications from indigenous people, women and /or candidates from a BME background for this vacancy as they are currently under-represented at this level in our department. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you have any questions on the application process, please email Alessandra Uomo, HR Coordinator, at hr@geog.cam.ac.uk . Informal enquiries should be directed to the Director of SPRI, Professor Richard Powell, at director@spri.cam.ac.uk . Interviews are expected in October/November 2026. Please quote reference LC49794 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Geography Salary £47,389-£59,966 Reference LC49794 Category Academic Date published 30 June 2026 Closing date 21 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    }
  },
